//! run
//! flags: -I ../../src/fort -I support
//! stdout:
//| ok
import lifetime;

// A build of a closure of two modules, as far as the module that `-S` stops
// at. The driver gives back everything it takes: the module set of the whole
// closure, the checker over it and the emitter. `-S` also spawns no clang for
// each round.
// D19.1, D20.5
//
// support/lifetime.ft states the rule of the probe, the two views and the
// size of a round. This file is one of six, one for each mode of the driver,
// because the harness kills a program at 60 s (toolchain.md 7.3) and the six
// modes in one program passed alone and timed out under load.
// T-094: one probe for each mode of the driver
//
// Verified by deletion, one release at a time, each one moving a view past
// lifetime.BOUND and turning this test red: the `defer
// containers.pool_free(&p)`, the `defer session.free(&s)` and the `defer
// analysis_release(&an, &analysis_live)` of driver.compile_entry; the `defer
// containers.pool_free(&p)` of driver.load_closure and the `defer
// strbuf.free(&b)` of driver.std_dir_beside; the symbol loop,
// `del(ck->syms)`, `del(ck->values)` and `types.type_table_free` of
// check.check_free; and `named`, `funcs`, `out`, `allocas`, `body`, `scopes`
// and the pool of gen.gen_free. This test is the only witness of
// `strbuf.free(&g->out)`. All 46 releases of the six modes were measured
// against all six tests.
// T-094: one probe for each mode of the driver
fn i32 main() {
    lifetime.write_runtime();
    lifetime.write_file("closure.ft",
        "import helper;\nfn i32 main() {\n    return helper.one();\n}\n");
    lifetime.write_file("helper.ft", "fn i32 one() {\n    return 1;\n}\n");
    string[3] mut build = {"fort", "-S", "closure.ft"};
    assert(lifetime.measure(build[0..3]) < lifetime.BOUND);
    println("ok");
    return 0;
}
